Myth: Before time learned to flow forward, there lived a clockmaker named Thane who built timepieces that ran backward. His creations showed only what had been, never what could be.

"Why do you trap yourself in yesterday?" asked a child passing his workshop.

Thane looked at his clocks, each one reversing toward moments he wished he could change. "I fear the future," he admitted. "At least the past is known."

The child placed a seed in his palm. "Plant this."

"It will only grow and die," Thane protested.

"Yes," the child smiled. "That is its gift."

